From: hurrhnn <hurrhnn@icmp.kr>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 03:00:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] xfrm: fragment oversized IPv4 packets before packet offload

A packet offload state hands the inner packet to the driver, and the
device adds the outer header and the ESP trailer. Before that,
xfrm_output() checks the inner packet with xfrm_dev_offload_ok(), which
refuses a packet that is longer than the inner MTU of the SA, the
xfrm_state_mtu() of the cached child MTU, and xfrm_output() frees it.
Only LINUX_MIB_XFRMOUTERROR counts the drop, and only with
XFRM_STATISTICS. No ICMP error is sent. A software ESP state encrypts
the same packet as a whole, and ip_output() fragments the outer packet
when DF is clear. With packet offload the device builds the outer
packet and may transmit it itself, so the stack cannot fragment it
afterwards.

On EN7581 with SOE packet offload, a LAN host sent 1500 byte UDP
datagrams through an IPv4 ESP tunnel as two fragments, because its path
MTU was lower. Conntrack reassembled them for forwarding, and every
reassembled datagram that was longer than the inner MTU of the SA was
freed silently. With a software SA the same datagrams reached the peer.
xfrm_output() also clears the IPv4 control block, so the size of the
original fragments is lost by the time the packet is refused.

RFC 4301 lets a security gateway fragment IPv4 packets before it
applies a tunnel mode SA (section 5.1). A packet that exceeds the PMTU
of an SA is to be fragmented when DF is clear and discarded with an
ICMP PMTU message when DF is set (section 8.2.1). Do this in
__xfrm4_output(), after the POSTROUTING hook and before xfrm_output(),
for a packet that is not GSO, has no IP options and is longer than the
MTU that xfrm_dev_offload_ok() checks, when the first state of the
bundle is a tunnel mode packet offload state without a nested
transform, bound to the device of the path, whose driver transmits
through xdo_dev_packet_xmit():

- A packet that must not be fragmented, by the rule of ip_fragment(),
  is dropped with -EMSGSIZE. A full socket gets the error through
  xfrm_local_error(), and any other sender ICMP FRAG_NEEDED carrying
  that MTU, as in xfrm4_tunnel_check_size(). ip_forward() already
  rejects most such forwarded packets against the MTU of the bundle.
- Any other packet gets frag_max_size lowered to that MTU and is
  fragmented with ip_do_fragment(). Each fragment is passed to
  xfrm_output() directly, so the hook does not run again, and fits the
  MTU that xfrm_dev_offload_ok() checks. A reassembled packet keeps the
  size of its original fragments if they were smaller.

A packet with IP options or for a state bound to another device is
refused by xfrm_dev_offload_ok() at any length, so it is left to that
check and does not get an MTU error it cannot act on.

Only a driver that transmits through xdo_dev_packet_xmit() encrypts
every packet it is given with the state that the stack selected.
Without that callback, xfrm_output() sends the plaintext packet to the
device, which matches it against its own policy and state rules. mlx5
installs its TX policy and SA rules for non-fragmented packets only, so
it would send the fragments unencrypted. Oversized packets for the
packet offload states of such drivers are still dropped.

The fragments are sent on the SA that was selected for the whole
packet. They are not looked up in the SPD again, although section 5.1
evaluates fragments made before IPsec against the SPD, and there is no
setting to turn this fragmentation off. A peer that checks non-initial
fragments against its inbound policy before reassembly accepts them
only on an SA whose port selectors are ANY, as described in RFC 4301
section 7.

Software and crypto offload states, transport, BEET and IPTFS mode
states, GSO packets and packets that fit are not changed. The offload
state is read under an offload reference, as xfrm_output() does, and
only for packets above the MTU.

IPv6 is not changed. __xfrm6_output() already fragments tunnel mode
packets that exceed the MTU of the bundle with ip6_fragment() before
xfrm_output(), for every state type.

Signed-off-by: hurrhnn <hurrhnn@icmp.kr>
---
--- a/net/ipv4/xfrm4_output.c
+++ b/net/ipv4/xfrm4_output.c
@@ -14,8 +14,92 @@
 #include <net/xfrm.h>
 #include <net/icmp.h>
 
+static int __xfrm4_output_finish(struct net *net, struct sock *sk,
+				 struct sk_buff *skb)
+{
+	return xfrm_output(sk, skb);
+}
+
+/* A packet offload device takes the inner packet and builds the outer
+ * packet itself, so the stack cannot fragment the outer packet as
+ * ip_output() does for a software state. xfrm_dev_offload_ok() refuses an
+ * inner packet that is longer than the inner MTU of the SA. Return that
+ * MTU if the packet has to be fragmented before it is handed to the
+ * device, 0 otherwise. IP options and a state bound to another device
+ * than the path make xfrm_dev_offload_ok() refuse a packet of any length,
+ * so such packets are left to it.
+ *
+ * Only a driver that transmits through xdo_dev_packet_xmit() encrypts
+ * every packet it is given with the state selected here. Without that
+ * callback the plaintext goes to the device, which matches it against
+ * its own policy and state rules. Those rules may skip IP fragments, as
+ * the mlx5 ones do, and the fragments would leave unencrypted.
+ */
+static unsigned int xfrm4_packet_offload_frag_mtu(struct sk_buff *skb)
+{
+#ifdef CONFIG_XFRM_OFFLOAD
+	struct dst_entry *dst = skb_dst(skb);
+	struct xfrm_state *x = dst->xfrm;
+	const struct xfrmdev_ops *ops;
+	bool packet_xmit;
+	unsigned int mtu;
+
+	if (skb_is_gso(skb) || x->props.mode != XFRM_MODE_TUNNEL ||
+	    dst_xfrm(xfrm_dst_child(dst)) || ip_hdr(skb)->ihl != 5)
+		return 0;
+
+	mtu = xfrm_state_mtu(x, ((struct xfrm_dst *)dst)->child_mtu_cached);
+	if (skb->len <= mtu || mtu < IPV4_MIN_MTU)
+		return 0;
+
+	/* The offload reference keeps the type, device and ops stable. */
+	if (!xfrm_dev_offload_get(x))
+		return 0;
+
+	ops = READ_ONCE(x->xso.state_ops);
+	packet_xmit = x->xso.type == XFRM_DEV_OFFLOAD_PACKET &&
+		      READ_ONCE(x->xso.dev) == xfrm_dst_path(dst)->dev &&
+		      ops && ops->xdo_dev_packet_xmit;
+	xfrm_dev_offload_put(x);
+
+	return packet_xmit ? mtu : 0;
+#else
+	return 0;
+#endif
+}
+
+/* RFC 4301 section 8.2.1: a packet that exceeds the MTU of the SA is
+ * fragmented if DF is clear, here before IPsec as section 5.1 allows, and
+ * discarded with an ICMP PMTU message if DF is set. DF is honoured as in
+ * ip_fragment().
+ */
+static int xfrm4_packet_offload_fragment(struct net *net, struct sock *sk,
+					 struct sk_buff *skb, unsigned int mtu)
+{
+	u16 frag_max_size = IPCB(skb)->frag_max_size;
+
+	if ((ip_hdr(skb)->frag_off & htons(IP_DF)) &&
+	    (!skb->ignore_df || frag_max_size > mtu)) {
+		IP_INC_STATS(net, IPSTATS_MIB_FRAGFAILS);
+		skb->protocol = htons(ETH_P_IP);
+		if (skb->sk && sk_fullsock(skb->sk))
+			xfrm_local_error(skb, mtu);
+		else
+			icmp_send(skb, ICMP_DEST_UNREACH, ICMP_FRAG_NEEDED,
+				  htonl(mtu));
+		kfree_skb_reason(skb, SKB_DROP_REASON_PKT_TOO_BIG);
+		return -EMSGSIZE;
+	}
+
+	if (!frag_max_size || frag_max_size > mtu)
+		IPCB(skb)->frag_max_size = mtu;
+
+	return ip_do_fragment(net, sk, skb, __xfrm4_output_finish);
+}
+
 static int __xfrm4_output(struct net *net, struct sock *sk, struct sk_buff *skb)
 {
+	unsigned int mtu;
 #ifdef CONFIG_NETFILTER
 	struct xfrm_state *x = skb_dst(skb)->xfrm;
 
@@ -25,6 +109,11 @@ static int __xfrm4_output(struct net *ne
 	}
 #endif
 
+	/* Decide before xfrm_output() clears the control block. */
+	mtu = xfrm4_packet_offload_frag_mtu(skb);
+	if (unlikely(mtu))
+		return xfrm4_packet_offload_fragment(net, sk, skb, mtu);
+
 	return xfrm_output(sk, skb);
 }
 
